/*! @page cursor_positioning Cursor positioning by fraction

The WT_CURSOR::set_position and WT_CURSOR::get_position methods describe
a cursor's place in an object as a fraction of the whole object, without
naming a key. An application can ask how far into an object a positioned
cursor is, or move a cursor to a given fraction of an object it knows
nothing about. Both methods are supported by row-store \c file:,
\c table: and \c layered: objects, including cursors opened on
checkpoints.

@section cursor_position_soft Soft positions

A position is a value in the range [0, 1] derived from the shape of the
in-memory tree: the root's children divide the range into equal parts,
each child divides its part among its own children, and so on down to the
records of a leaf page. Positions preserve key order (a record with a
larger key never has a smaller position), but they are not uniform: two
positions the same distance apart can span very different numbers of
records.

Positions also change as the object changes. Pages split, merge, are
evicted and are read again, and the in-memory tree differs from the tree
on disk. A position is therefore soft: it is valid for sampling and for
progress reporting, and it can be used to resume a best-effort scan, but
it must never be stored as a durable pointer to a record. Use a key for
that.

@section cursor_position_struct The WT_POSITION structure

WT_CURSOR::set_position takes a pointer to a WT_POSITION structure. A
zero-filled structure selects the default behavior, so an application
fills in only the fields it needs:

- WT_POSITION::pos is the requested position. Values below 0 address
  the first page of the object and values above 1 the last page.
- WT_POSITION::flags selects the mode, the walk direction and the
  anchor, as described below. Unknown bits are ignored.
- WT_POSITION::pages_skipped is an output field: the number of pages
  stepped over because the page the position addressed could not be
  used. Zero means the addressed page itself was used. The count is
  approximate: a step over an unreadable subtree counts once. The field
  is set on success and on ::WT_NOTFOUND.

Fields may be appended to the structure in later versions.

@section cursor_position_default Positioning the cursor

By default WT_CURSOR::set_position reads the leaf page the position
addresses, selects a starting slot on that page, and moves forwards to
the first record visible to the cursor's transaction. On success the
cursor is positioned with its key and value set, exactly as after
WT_CURSOR::search_near, and WT_CURSOR::next, WT_CURSOR::prev and the
other cursor methods continue from there. If no visible record follows,
::WT_NOTFOUND is returned and the cursor is reset.

@snippet ex_all.c Position a cursor at a fraction of the object

The starting slot is chosen by the anchor flags: by default
(::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_EXACT) the remainder of the position selects a slot
within the page, ::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_FIRST selects the first slot,
::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_MIDDLE the middle slot and ::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_LAST
the last. Only records already written to the page are indexed this way;
records still on the page's insert list are visited by the walk but are
never selected as a starting slot. A page with no records written to it
yet, which is the usual state of a never-checkpointed object, has no
slots to select: the walk starts on its insert list, at the smallest key
or, with ::WT_POSITION_PREV, the largest.

::WT_POSITION_PREV walks backwards instead of forwards. The anchor and
the direction are independent: ::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_LAST alone starts at
the last slot and moves forwards, which reaches the next page if that
slot is not visible, while ::WT_POSITION_ANCHOR_LAST combined with
::WT_POSITION_PREV returns the last visible on-disk slot of the
addressed page.

If the addressed page cannot be used, because it was deleted, is locked,
or is not in memory in cache-only mode, the nearest usable page in the
walk direction is used instead and WT_POSITION::pages_skipped reports how
many pages were stepped over. On an intact, fully readable object the
field is always zero.

@section cursor_position_get Reading the cursor's position

WT_CURSOR::get_position returns the position of the record the cursor is
positioned on. The cursor must be positioned, otherwise EINVAL is
returned; a cursor that only has a key set, including the key set by
key-only positioning, is not positioned.

@snippet ex_all.c Report progress with the cursor's position

The two methods are consistent with each other. For an unchanged object,
positioning at the value WT_CURSOR::get_position returned for a record
written to a page returns the same record. A record that is still on a
page's insert list maps to the nearest slot written to the page, so the
round trip may return a neighboring record instead.

@section cursor_position_key_only Key-only positioning

With ::WT_POSITION_KEY_ONLY no leaf page is read. The cursor's key is set
to the boundary key of the page the position addresses, as if
WT_CURSOR::set_key had been called, and the cursor is left unpositioned.
The boundary key is the separator stored in the parent page: it is
greater than every key on the preceding page and no greater than the
first key on its own page, and it aligns with a page edge, so a range
ending at it can be truncated without reading the pages inside.

@snippet ex_all.c Find page boundary keys without reading leaf pages

Boundary keys need care:

- They are usually not the key of any record. Reconciliation shortens
  separators to the shortest prefix that still separates the two pages.
  A string-format (\c S) boundary is a valid, shorter string and is
  returned as such by WT_CURSOR::get_key; a boundary of another typed
  format such as \c q, \c Q or \c i may not unpack. Read those through a
  cursor opened in @ref cursor_raw "raw mode" and treat them as opaque
  byte strings that sort correctly against packed keys.
  WT_CURSOR::search_near from the key works regardless of format and
  reaches the first visible record of the page.
- The leftmost page of the object has the empty key as its boundary. The
  empty key is a sentinel meaning the start of the object: it is not
  usable as a search key or as a cursor bound, and on an object with a
  custom collator it does not necessarily sort first. Non-empty
  boundaries are prefixes of real keys and compare correctly under any
  collator. An empty object, or one with no internal pages, such as one
  that has never been checkpointed and still fits in a single in-memory
  page, yields the empty key for every position.
- The anchor and direction flags have no effect in this mode, and
  WT_POSITION::pages_skipped is always zero, because the state of the
  leaf pages does not matter.

Key-only positioning costs a descent through the internal pages only,
which are a small fraction of the object and are usually cached, and
performs no I/O in the common case.

@section cursor_position_cache_only Cache-only positioning

::WT_POSITION_CACHE_ONLY confines the call to pages already in memory: no
page is read from disk and no locked page is waited for. If the addressed
page is not in memory, the nearest page in the walk direction that is in
memory is used and WT_POSITION::pages_skipped is set; if nothing usable
is in memory, ::WT_NOTFOUND is returned. Overflow items of the returned
record are still read from disk, and on tables with several column
groups the flag applies to the primary column group only. This suits
diagnostics and heuristics that want to look at resident data without
causing I/O.

@snippet ex_all.c Position only within cached pages

@section cursor_position_layered Layered tables

A layered table's positions are defined over its stable constituent when
the table has one, and over its ingest constituent otherwise. Records
present only in the other constituent are not part of the position
space, but they are returned by WT_CURSOR::next and WT_CURSOR::prev after
positioning as usual. A stable record that the ingest constituent has
removed or hidden resolves to its nearest visible neighbor, as
WT_CURSOR::search_near would. That resolution may read neighboring pages,
so ::WT_POSITION_CACHE_ONLY is best-effort on layered tables.

WT_CURSOR::get_position on a layered cursor positioned on an ingest-only
record reports the position of the nearest stable record, and the cursor
keeps its place. If the stable constituent is empty the method returns
::WT_NOTFOUND.

@section cursor_position_errors Unsupported cursors and errors

- Column-store objects return ENOTSUP from both methods.
- Cursors with bounds set, cursors configured with \c next_random and
  bulk cursors return EINVAL from WT_CURSOR::set_position.
- A NaN position returns EINVAL.
- An empty object returns ::WT_NOTFOUND from WT_CURSOR::set_position,
  except in key-only mode, which returns the empty key.
- Index cursors and cursors on other data sources (statistics, backup,
  metadata, log and so on) return ENOTSUP.

@section cursor_position_uses Common uses

Sampling an object at evenly spaced positions replaces a random cursor
with a deterministic walk: positioning at \c i/N for \c i from 0 to \c N
returns the samples in key order, reading one leaf page per sample in
the common case. The walk to a visible record may continue onto a
neighboring page, a table with several column groups searches each of
them for the record, and a layered table resolves the record again
through its constituents.

@snippet ex_all.c Partition an object into evenly spaced samples

The same loop in key-only mode yields \c N-1 page-aligned boundary keys
without reading any leaf page, which is what a range-based truncation
policy or a job that splits an object into ranges for parallel workers
needs: worker \c k scans from boundary \c k inclusive to boundary \c k+1
exclusive.

A long scan can report its progress by calling WT_CURSOR::get_position
every so often, without counting the records first. A best-effort
background pass can save the position when it pauses and later resume
with WT_CURSOR::set_position followed by WT_CURSOR::next; because
positions are soft, a few records near the pause point may be visited
twice or skipped if the tree changed in between, so this idiom suits
statistics gathering, not anything that must visit each record exactly
once.

 */
